ellham Power is the largest single buyer. It plans 2027 capital spending up 18% and has a transformer tender of about C$900M due to award in Q1 2027.\n- Norvane Utilities plans spending up 12% and has a C$450M tender due in Q2 2027.\n- Both October capital plans are mostly substations and transformers.\n- VLTX won 60% of the last tender round, so it is well placed to benefit.\n\n**Data centres (rising, but slower)**\n- The `datacentre-demand` outlook expects grid connection requests to grow 9% in 2027. That is slower than 2026 because power availability limits new sites.\n- Connection requests are only a proxy for equipment demand. I didn't find or read the connection queue report the outlook points to, so I have no breakdown by equipment type, timing or site.\n\n**Caveats**\n- Planned capital spending is not the same as awarded orders. The utility plans only turn into orders if the utilities can finance them.\n- On financing, n6's finding is that the equity window looks effectively shut. That is a risk to the plans, and n3 is still assessing it.\n- There are no 2026 dollar baselines, only growth rates.\n- I found nothing on utilities other than Pellham and Norvane.\n- I did not check how much VLTX orders depend on these two buyers.\n\nConfidence is moderate on direction and low on size.\n\nThe detail is in n7.r1 (utilities) and n8.r1 (data centres).","ttft_ms":3812,"type":"result","duration_ms":45876,"uuid":"bf414c9e-0931-46e1-8448-2f1cd75dfb41","ttft_stream_ms":2609,"time_to_request_ms":633,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":37,"system_prompt":20,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":13,"input_attachments":77,"input_hooks":14,"conversation_append":8,"transcript_persist":36,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":80,"init_frame_send":22,"engine_pickup":2,"loop_entry":47,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":29,"request_announcements":141,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":26,"message_normalization":21,"client_creation":32,"request_bui